# Authentication API

Overview
This page documents the server's authentication endpoints and recommended secure implementation practices for developers.

Endpoints
1) POST /api/v1/auth/login
- Request (application/json):
  {
    "email": "user@example.com",
    "password": "plain-text-password"
  }
- Responses:
  - 200 OK
    {
      "access_token": "<JWT>",
      "refresh_token": "<opaque>",
      "token_type": "Bearer",
      "expires_in": 900
    }
    Notes:
      - access_token: JWT (see JWT section below) valid for 15 minutes (900 seconds).
      - refresh_token: opaque token valid for 7 days. Store securely server-side (see storage guidance).
      - expires_in: seconds until access token expiration.
  - 400 Bad Request
    {
      "errors": {
        "email": "Invalid email",
        "password": "Required"
      }
    }
    Use structured field-level error objects for validation errors.
  - 401 Unauthorized
    {
      "error": "Invalid credentials"
    }
    Use a generic message — do not reveal whether the email exists.
  - 429 Too Many Requests
    {
      "error": "Too many failed login attempts. Try again later."
    }
    Include Retry-After header where applicable.

2) POST /api/v1/auth/refresh
- Request (application/json):
  {
    "refresh_token": "<opaque token>"
  }
- Responses:
  - 200 OK: returns a new access_token (and optionally a rotated refresh_token)
    {
      "access_token": "<new JWT>",
      "refresh_token": "<new opaque>"  // optional; if present, rotate refresh tokens
      "token_type": "Bearer",
      "expires_in": 900
    }
  - 400 / 401: invalid or expired refresh token

JWT (access_token) guidance
- JWT format: three base64url-encoded parts separated by dots.
- Signing:
  - HS256 or RS256 supported. Prefer RS256 (asymmetric) for key separation when feasible.
  - If using HS256, securely manage and rotate the HMAC secret.
  - If using RS256, publish the signing public key via secure key distribution or JWKS if using multiple services.
- Required claims:
  - sub: user id (string or numeric; normalize to string in docs)
  - iat: issued-at (numeric, epoch seconds)
  - exp: expiration timestamp (iat + 900)
  - roles: array of role strings (optional, include if any)
- Verify signature and exp on protected endpoints; reject tokens with invalid signature or expired exp.

Token lifetimes
- access_token: 15 minutes (900 seconds).
- refresh_token: 7 days by default. Consider refresh token rotation and single-use tokens for stronger security.

Security and storage recommendations
- Passwords:
  - Store only bcrypt-hashed passwords (bcrypt with a work factor appropriate for your environment).
  - On login, compare bcrypt(hash, provided_password) using a constant-time comparison function provided by your crypto library.
- Refresh tokens:
  - Store refresh tokens server-side (database) with these fields: token hash (store hashed version), user_id, issued_at, expires_at, revoked (boolean), last_used_at.
  - When issuing a refresh token, store only a hashed representation (e.g., HMAC or bcrypt) so the raw token is not in DB.
  - Prefer refresh-token rotation: when a refresh is used, issue a new refresh token and mark the old token revoked. If a revoked token is used, treat as potential theft — revoke all tokens for the user and require re-authentication.
  - Invalidate refresh tokens on password change or manual logout (mark revoked).
- SQL injection:
  - Always use parameterized queries or an ORM's parameter binding (do NOT concatenate user input into SQL).
  - Example (pseudo-SQL parameterized):
    SELECT id, password_hash FROM users WHERE email = $1;
  - Log query parameters separately from SQL; trace logs should not show raw secrets.

Error handling and privacy
- Return generic error messages for authentication failures to avoid user enumeration.
- Record failed attempts (with rate-limiting) but avoid logging sensitive fields like passwords.

Rate limiting / brute-force mitigation (brief guidance)
- Apply per-account and per-IP failed-attempt tracking.
- Example policy: after 5 failed attempts within 15 minutes for an account or IP, return 429 with Retry-After header; or temporarily lock account for a short period (e.g., 15 minutes).
- Ensure lockout decisions are logged and reviewable.

Testing guidance
- Unit tests:
  - Seed a test user with bcrypt hash and known id/roles.
  - Test correct login path: response contains access_token (three parts), refresh_token, correct token_type and expires_in.
  - Decode JWT and verify sub, iat, exp, roles.
- Expiry testing:
  - In test environments, create tokens with exp in the past or allow test clock manipulation to simulate expiry.
  - Verify protected endpoints return 401 for expired access tokens.
- Refresh flow:
  - Test refresh endpoint returns a new access_token and rotates refresh_token if rotation is enabled.
  - Test reusing a rotated refresh_token (if single-use) is rejected.

Notes
- This document is a reference for implementing the auth endpoints. Implementation details (libraries, frameworks) will vary by tech stack — follow the security guidance above when mapping to your stack.

