# Authentication Security Best Practices

Purpose
This page expands on secure implementation details referenced by the Authentication API doc.

Password storage
- Use bcrypt with a cost/work factor appropriate to your deployment (e.g., bcrypt cost 12+ depending on CPU).
- Never store plaintext passwords.
- Use libs that perform constant-time comparisons when verifying hashes.

Refresh token storage and rotation
- Store a hashed version of the refresh token in DB (do not store raw tokens).
- Table columns example:
  - id (PK), user_id, token_hash, issued_at, expires_at, revoked (boolean), last_used_at, ip_address, user_agent
- On refresh:
  - Validate token_hash and expiry.
  - If valid and rotation enabled:
    - create new refresh token, hash & store it, mark old token revoked and set last_used_at.
    - return new refresh token to client.
  - If token invalid or revoked: reject and consider revoking all refresh tokens for the user (possible token compromise).

Preventing SQL injection
- Always use parameterized queries or ORM parameter binding. Example (Node/pg):
  const text = 'SELECT id, password_hash FROM users WHERE email = $1';
  const values = [email];
  client.query(text, values);
- Avoid building SQL using string concatenation with user input.
- Use prepared statements where possible.

Logging and monitoring
- Log authentication failures (timestamp, user identifier if available, source IP), but do not log raw passwords or raw refresh tokens.
- Monitor failed-auth spikes and anomalous refresh-token activity.

Key management
- For HS256: rotate the shared secret periodically and provide key identifiers if multiple keys are in use.
- For RS256: rotate signing keys and publish current public keys via a JWKS endpoint for service-to-service verification.

